ci: Use a CMake nightly build and treat linker warnings as errors in the macOS cross job

The "macOS-cross to arm64" CI configuration is run with a CMake nightly
build, which the workflow unpacks into `cmake-snapshot` in the source
tree, and with `-DCMAKE_LINK_WARNING_AS_ERROR=ON`.

`-DCMAKE_INSTALL_NAME_TOOL=true` is passed as well, as `depends` does
for Qt. The tool is not used when cross-compiling for macOS, yet CMake
fails the configuration if it is not found. Bitcoin Core works around
that for CMake < 4.2 only. CMake >= 4.2 also accepts
`llvm-install-name-tool`, which the container image does not provide
under that name.

`PATH` is exported from the environment file, because the CI scripts
pass to the container only the variables that such files export. The
value seen in the container therefore includes the runner's own `PATH`,
whose extra entries do not exist there.

Applied by .github/workflows/macos-cross.yml.

diff --git a/ci/test/00_setup_env_mac_cross.sh b/ci/test/00_setup_env_mac_cross.sh
index c823524..770f1fb 100755
--- a/ci/test/00_setup_env_mac_cross.sh
+++ b/ci/test/00_setup_env_mac_cross.sh
@@ -17,8 +17,13 @@ export OSX_SDK_SHA256=9600fa93644df674ee916b5e2c8a6ba8dacf631996a65dc922d003b98b
 export RUN_UNIT_TESTS=false
 export RUN_FUNCTIONAL_TESTS=false
 export BUILD_TARGETS="all deploy"
+# The workflow unpacks a CMake nightly build into the source tree, which the CI
+# scripts copy to BASE_ROOT_DIR inside the container.
+export PATH="${BASE_ROOT_DIR}/cmake-snapshot/bin:${PATH}"
 printf -v BITCOIN_CONFIG "%q " \
  --preset=dev-mode \
  -DWITH_USDT=OFF \
- -DREDUCE_EXPORTS=ON
+ -DREDUCE_EXPORTS=ON \
+ -DCMAKE_LINK_WARNING_AS_ERROR=ON \
+ -DCMAKE_INSTALL_NAME_TOOL=true
 export BITCOIN_CONFIG
